# What the analyses write, which is a result and not a checked-in artifact.
#
# `pc test` runs every scenario of every analysis, and `Shape.analyze_async()`
# writes the model each produced beside the part --
# `cantilever.fea.tip-load.glb` here, and `<part>.<analysis>.glb` for a section
# still written as one unnamed scenario. The `Examples (PartCAD)`
# job then runs `pc render -r` and fails if anything under `examples/` changed,
# which is how a rendering regression is caught; an untracked solver result
# tripped that check the first time the solver actually ran on a runner.
#
# It is not checked in, and should not be: the images beside it are PartCAD's
# own byte-stable renderings, while this is whatever the solver and the mesher
# produced on that machine, at those versions. Committing it would make the
# check fail on the next release of either.
*.fea.glb
*.fea.*.glb
*.dynamic.glb
*.dynamic.*.glb
*.thermal.glb
*.thermal.*.glb
*.cfd.glb
*.cfd.*.glb
